```css
nav {
  display: flex;
  flex-wrap: wrap;
  gap: 12px;
  justify-content: space-between;
}
```
That's a genuinely complete, responsive-ish nav row in four lines: items in a row, allowed to wrap onto a second line if they don't fit, evenly spaced, with consistent gaps. It won't handle every layout case, but it covers a surprising share of the real ones you'll actually build.

contentSections.synthesis:
```css
nav { display: flex; flex-direction: row; gap: 12px; }
@media (max-width: 480px) {
  nav { flex-direction: column; }
}
```
The base rule is the default, everywhere. The media query overrides just one property, just below 480px — the nav is a horizontal row until the screen gets narrow, then becomes a stacked column, with no separate mobile page to maintain.

