```js
form.addEventListener('submit', function(event) {
  event.preventDefault();
  const pain = Number(document.querySelector('#painLevel').value);
  console.log('Logged pain level:', pain);
});
```
`event.preventDefault()` is the general tool for stopping a browser's built-in default reaction to an event — a form's default submit-and-reload, or a link's default navigate-away. It doesn't stop the event itself from being handled by your code; it only cancels whatever the browser would otherwise have done automatically afterward.

```js
const detailsBtn = document.querySelector('#detailsBtn');
const detailsPanel = document.querySelector('#detailsPanel');
detailsBtn.addEventListener('click', function() {
  detailsPanel.classList.toggle('hidden');
});
```
with `.hidden { display: none; }` defined in CSS. `.classList.toggle('hidden')` adds the class if it's missing and removes it if it's present — flipping between shown and hidden with each click, using `display: none` as the actual hiding mechanism so the panel is consistently gone for everyone when it's supposed to be.

```js
detailsBtn.addEventListener('click', function() {
  const isHidden = detailsPanel.classList.toggle('hidden');
  detailsBtn.setAttribute('aria-expanded', String(!isHidden));
  detailsBtn.textContent = isHidden ? 'Show details' : 'Hide details';
});
```
`classList.toggle()` conveniently returns `true` if the class ended up *added* (meaning: now hidden), so that return value can drive both the aria-expanded attribute and the button's own label in the same handler, kept perfectly in sync because they're set from the exact same toggle.

```js
localStorage.setItem('latestNote', 'Feeling steadier today');
const note = localStorage.getItem('latestNote');
console.log(note); // "Feeling steadier today", even after a reload
```
There's also `sessionStorage`, with the exact same setItem/getItem methods, but scoped to one browser tab — it clears the moment that tab is closed, unlike localStorage which survives closing the whole browser. Use localStorage for anything that should genuinely persist (a saved draft, a theme preference); sessionStorage for anything that should only last as long as the current visit.

