contentSections.body:
```js
const saved = localStorage.getItem('entries');
let entries;
try {
  entries = saved ? JSON.parse(saved) : [];
} catch (error) {
  console.log('Saved data was corrupted, starting fresh:', error);
  entries = [];
}
```
`try { ... }` runs code that might throw; if it does, execution jumps immediately to `catch (error) { ... }` instead of crashing the whole script, with `error` holding details about what went wrong. Combined with lesson 3's null check, this handles all three real states a saved value can be in: never saved (null), saved and valid (real data), and saved but corrupted (a caught error) — each with its own deliberate, non-crashing outcome.

```js
fetch('https://api.example.com/current?city=Boston')
  .then(function(response) {
    console.log(response);
