contentSections.body:
**async/await** is the syntax for actually pausing at a specific point until a Promise resolves:

```js
async function loadWeather() {
  const response = await fetch('https://api.example.com/current?city=Boston');
  const data = await response.json();
  console.log(data.temperature); // the real value, correctly waited for
}
```
`await` pauses execution of *this specific function* at that line, until the Promise resolves — the next line only runs once the real response (or real parsed data) is actually available. This produces the exact same end result as the `.then()` chains from the last lesson, just written to read top-to-bottom like ordinary, sequential code.

contentSections.flawExplanation:
The fix is wrapping this code in a function marked `async`, then calling that function:

```js
async function loadWeather() {
  const data = await fetch('https://api.example.com/current?city=Boston');
  console.log(data);
}
loadWeather();
```
`async` on the function declaration is what makes `await` legal inside its body — the two keywords are a pair, and one doesn't work meaningfully without the other.

contentSections.body:
```js
async function loadWeather() {
  try {
    const response = await fetch('https://api.example.com/current?city=Boston');
    const data = await response.json();
    console.log(data.temperature);
  } catch (error) {
    console.log('Failed to load weather:', error);
    // show a real message to the user here
  }
}
```
This is the exact same try/catch pattern from the storing-data module — wrapping risky, possibly-failing code, and recovering deliberately instead of letting an uncaught error crash the surrounding script. `catch` here handles genuine network failures: no connection, a timed-out request, the server not responding at all.

