contentSections.body:
```js
async function loadWeather() {
  try {
    const response = await fetch('https://api.example.com/current?city=Boston');
    if (!response.ok) {
      console.log('Server responded with an error:', response.status);
      return;
    }
    const data = await response.json();
    console.log(data.temperature);
  } catch (error) {
    console.log('Network failure:', error);
  }
}
```
Two separate checks, for two separate failure modes: `catch` for the request never completing at all, and `if (!response.ok)` for the request completing but the server reporting an error. A page that only has one of these will handle exactly half of what can actually go wrong.
